WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.580
Suppose there is a fence erected in the middle
of nowhere that’s blocking your path: what

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:10.620
do you do? Do you take it down in order to
continue on your way? Or do you first think

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:13.080
hard about why it is there to begin with?

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:23.820
Chesterton's Fence is a simple rule of thumb
that suggests that you should never destroy

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:29.580
a fence, change a rule, or do away with a
tradition until you understand why it’s

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:30.780
there in the first place.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:38.700
The principle assumes that fences have a purpose,
were carefully planned, and cost time and

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:43.740
money to erect. Someone must have had a reason
for thinking that a fence would be a good

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:46.920
idea. So what if we just take them down?

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:54.360
Entrepreneur Steve Blank likes to point out
that when startups get their first Chief Financial

00:00:54.360 --> 00:01:00.660
Officer, the newly hired often wants to show
that they are proactive and cut costs. Some

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:05.940
go as far as calculating the expense of the
free snacks offered to employees and then

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:08.280
start charging the staff a small fee.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:15.720
People can of course pay, but they don’t
like this change in company culture — and

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:22.320
many may quit. As a result, the firm makes
pennies on the nuts but spends thousands hiring

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:28.440
new engineers. All because the reformer didn’t
understand the purpose of the policy he axed.

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:36.180
A more tragic example of misguided reform
was China’s so-called Great Leap Forward,

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:43.680
which lasted from 1958 to 1962. One of
its initiatives was the Four Pests Campaign

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:50.220
to eliminate all mosquitoes, flies, rats,
and even sparrows that were suspected of stealing

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:51.480
grain from the fields.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:59.820
And so millions of people hit noisy pots and
pans to prevent sparrows from resting, with

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:05.460
the goal of causing them to drop dead from
exhaustion. Some simply shot the birds down

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:09.420
from the sky. Others destroyed their nests
and broke their eggs.

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:17.700
The campaign worked and pushed the Chinese
sparrow population to near extinction. So

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:19.800
what about the unintended consequences?

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:28.800
With no sparrows to eat them, locust populations
ballooned, swarming the country and compounding

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:34.500
the problems caused by other reforms. The
consequence was the Great Chinese Famine,

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:40.320
one of the largest man-made disasters in human
history, with a death toll in the tens of

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:40.820
millions.

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:46.200
Perhaps all those who want&nbsp;
to break with traditions,&nbsp;

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:49.260
or reform norms, should learn to recite this

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:54.420
short line from philosopher F. K. Chestertons’
original 1929 writing:

00:02:56.100 --> 00:03:02.640
“In the matter of reforming things, as distinct
from deforming them, there is one plain and

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:03.660
simple principle.”

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:11.880
Let us say, for the sake of simplicity, a
fence or gate is erected across a road. The

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:17.040
more modern type of reformer goes up to it
and says, 'I don't see the use of this; let

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:22.260
us clear it away.' To which, the more intelligent
type of reformer will do well to answer:

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:28.740
'If you don't see the use of it, I certainly
won't let you clear it away. Go away and think.

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:34.020
Then, when you can come back and tell me that
you do see the use of it, I may allow you

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:34.860
to destroy it.”

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:43.200
What do you think? Is Chesterton's defense
of conservatism a good rule to live by? Or

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:48.120
do you also need people to break down old
structures even if they don’t fully understand

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:53.280
why they were built in the first place? Share
your thoughts in the comments below!

